            <ArticleTitle>Anti-inflammatory and antinociceptive activity of diphenyl diselenide.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>56-63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE AND DESIGN" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Ebselen, an organoselenium compound is able to modulate the inflammatory response in rodents. In the present study, the anti-inflammatory and antinociceptive activity of diaryl diselenides and ebselen was studied.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Adult male Wistar rats and albino mice were treated with diaryl diselenides and ebselen in different doses.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Carrageenin-induced paw edema, tail-flick, formalin, acetic acid-induced abdominal writhing and capsaicin models of pain were carried out. Data were analyzed by ANOVA followed by Duncan's multiple range when appropriate.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In all models, the most promising profile was displayed by diphenyl diselenide, which produced anti-inflammatory and antinociceptive activity significantly higher than ebselen. Diphenyl diselenide also produced dose-dependent antinociception when assessed in acetic acid-induced abdominal constriction, tail-flick test or formalin and capsaicin-induced nociception.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The data presented here provide evidence that administration of diphenyl diselenide produced anti-inflammatory and antinociceptive activity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
